+		<English>[H1]Military Advisor Summary Legend[\H1][PARAGRAPH:2]The Summary tab consolidates support cost, army composition / quality, and deployment data at a glance. Most numbers were already in Civ4 but scattered across the Finance hover, Composition tab, and city screens; this tab brings the headline data into one panel and exposes the actual DLL formulas so "why is my cost X?" reads directly instead of by guesswork.[PARAGRAPH:2][H2]Support Column - Unit Cost Formula[\H2][NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Total Units / Free Used/Cap / Paid: total owned units, free units used out of the free-unit cap (handicap + base + population %), and paid units (count beyond the cap).[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]P x R x M: regular unit cost = Paid x getGoldPerUnit() x getUnitCostMultiplier() / 10000. K-Mod stores the rates as percent-like integers so the visible product can floor to 0 even with positive paid units.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Mil. Cost Units / No Mil. Cost Units: subset of Total Units that pays / bypasses the military surcharge (bMilitarySupport flag in CIV4UnitInfos.xml). Some units, civilian or military, have no military cost (e.g. as of now Worker, Robotic Infantry - see their Sevopedia entry).[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Mil. Cost P x R: military surcharge = Paid Military x getGoldPerMilitaryUnit() / 100. Stacks on top of regular unit cost.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Extra Cost: getExtraUnitCost() / 100, per-unit overrides.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]U Cost x I: pre-inflation unit cost x inflation factor = displayed Unit Cost.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Outside Units: units outside own territory. Free / Paid threshold from INITIAL_FREE_OUTSIDE_UNITS define plus game-speed adjustments.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Outside P x R: Paid Outside x INITIAL_OUTSIDE_UNIT_GOLD_PERCENT / 100. AI handicap can apply an extra multiplier shown as raw then adjusted.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]U Supply x I: pre-inflation unit supply x inflation factor = displayed Unit Supply.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Total Cost + Supply: Unit Cost + Unit Supply, the bottom-line gold/turn.[PARAGRAPH:2][H2]Support Column - City Stats[\H2][NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Military Buildings: counts of Barracks / Stables / Drydocks built / total cities (Drydocks denominator is coastal cities). Heroic Epic / Military Academy show the owning city name or "-" if not built. The XP suffix on Barracks (+3), Stables (+X Mounted), Drydocks (+X Naval) is read directly from the building XML.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Unit Production: Avg Modifier averages getMilitaryProductionModifier() across cities; Best City highlights the strongest single bonus. Avg New XP / Best New XP read getFreeExperience() per city (Barracks XP, civics, religion, traits all stack into it).[PARAGRAPH:2][H2]Army Column[\H2][NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Military Units / Civilian Units: split by canFight() (combat-capable). Civilian units (Workers, Settlers, Spies, Missionaries, Great People) still count toward regular Unit Cost.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Land / Sea / Air: military units split by getDomainType().[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Strongest: unit type with the highest base XML strength (or air combat for air units). Costliest: highest base hammer cost.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Total Hammers / Avg Hammers: sum and mean of getProductionCost() across military units. Hammer cost works as an alternative power proxy alongside base strength, since Civ4 layers combat modifiers (collateral, city attack, withdraw, first strikes) that base strength alone does not reflect. For example, Trebuchet has lower base strength than concurrent melee units of its era but stronger city-attack value, which the hammer cost prices in.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Power Rank (Known): your getPower() rank among met civs.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Avg XP / Avg Level: averaged across military units only; civilians don't dilute.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Great Generals: standalone GG units (UnitInfo.getLeaderPromotion() set) waiting to attach. GG-Led Units: combat units carrying PROMOTION_LEADER after a prior attach.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Top Promotions: 5 most common promotions across your military, with count and %-of-military. Icon next to each row.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Covert Units: counts of units with getInvisibleType() (submarines, etc.), isSpy() (spies), isHiddenNationality() (privateers). Named "covert" rather than "special" because Civ4 already uses "special" for a distinct concept (special unit classes, special buildings). Always shown so the section's existence is discoverable; a zero state is informative.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Upgradeable: count of units with at least one available upgrade you can build. Min/Avg/Max/Total Cost: cheapest upgrade prices summed across upgradeable units.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Allied Hammers: total hammer cost across allied civs' military (teammates + mutual vassalage). Allies are "semi-ours" strategically; counts your shared force.[PARAGRAPH:2][H2]Deployment Column[\H2][NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]In Own Cities: count + Best Defended (city with highest getBuildingDefense()) and Avg Defense (mean across own cities).[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]In Own Territory: in own borders but not in a city.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]In Allied / Neutral Civ / Enemy Territory: foreign-civ plot owners split by diplomatic relation. Allied = same team or mutual vassalage. Enemy = at war. Neutral = any other met civ (open-borders partners and defensive-pact partners land here).[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Roaming (Unowned Land) / At Sea (Open Water): unowned plots, land vs water.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Defenses: counts of Walls / Castles built / total cities, with building icons.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Wounded: count of military units with any damage, + % of military.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Max / Avg / Min Health: hit-points-based health %; colored 100% = white, above 66% = green, above 33% = yellow, else red. Same thresholds as the Battles tab strength coloring.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Full / High / Medium / Low Health: distribution by band (count + % of military). Band rows are intentionally not colored - the values are counts, not health percentages.[PARAGRAPH:2][H2]Deployment Column - Battle Record[\H2][NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Battles / Won / Retreated / Lost: aggregated from the persisted SASBattleHistory log (same data feeding the Battles tab). Outcome icons match the Battles tab (white star = won, left arrow = retreated, broken heart = lost).[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Good-Luck Wins: wins where our pre-battle odds were below 50% - we were the underdog and won anyway.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Bad-Luck Losses: losses where our pre-battle odds were above 50% - we were favored and lost.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Best: the win with the lowest pre-battle odds (most underdog). Worst: the loss with the highest pre-battle odds (most expected to win).[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Both Best/Worst rows show the two unit icons (ours + theirs), starting effective strengths, year, and final odds %.[PARAGRAPH:2][H2]Color Coding[\H2][NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Money lines (Unit Cost / Unit Supply / Total Cost + Supply): green = 0 cost, red = positive cost. Earlier "yellow if income still net-positive" coloring was dropped because this panel explains where the burden comes from, not whether it's affordable in isolation.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Paid count rows: default white (just a count, "paid" doesn't mean "unaffordable").[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Health %: 100 = white, above 66 = green, above 33 = yellow, else red.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Battle outcomes: green = won, yellow = retreated, red = lost.[PARAGRAPH:2][H2]Indent / Icon Conventions[\H2][NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Sub-rows step right under their parent ("Free / Paid" under "Total Units"). The right-aligned value steps inward too, so a parent's number sits visually above where its children sum to.[NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]Icons sit at the row's right edge; the value text wraps to their left. Two-icon rows (Best / Worst battles) pair our unit + their unit.[PARAGRAPH:2][H2]Perspective[\H2][NEWLINE][ICON_BULLET]The player dropdown selects whose army is summarized. Outside debug mode this is constrained to you and your vassals (when the SAS define allows). All values are computed from the engine's state for that civ, with no fog-of-war filtering.
+	</English>
